        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:44.550Z"
      }
    },
    "c12": {
      "anchor": "4-writing#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "4. Changing, undoing, healing",
      "excerpt": "A change is one field of one thing, kept on the device, sent when the action tha",
      "text": "there should also be an option to e.g. fill out an entire form with a save button, wha'ts the language for this?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:11:11.151Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "sends several together, or none",
      "offset": 107,
      "edited": "2026-11-05T13:11:36.244Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:32:09.418Z",
          "html": "<p>Today:  <code>&lt;form value={a draft of the recipe}&gt;</code>, then <code>save the draft</code> or <code>discard the draft</code> (one group, one Undo).  A shorter way, the form making the draft itself: <a href=\"/epics/data/data.plan.html#q36\">Q36</a> (recommended:  <code>&lt;form edits={its recipe}&gt;</code>, its submit button saving).</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:45.587Z"
      }
    },
    "c13": {
      "anchor": "9-spellml#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "9. spellml's own tags",
      "excerpt": "spellml's notes take its prefix: <spell-description> , <spell-comment> <spell-pr",
      "text": "this doesn't belong in this guide, has nothing to do with spaces",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:14:51.431Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "spellml's notes take its prefix:",
      "offset": 0,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:29:21.703Z",
          "html": "<p>Moved: the section is gone from this guide, and its facts (the Spanish tags, the warning on an old short note, the test on short names) are now a sub-section of the spellml guide, \"spellml's own notes take its prefix\", in Writing a template in tags. The sections after it are numbered one lower.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:46.631Z"
      }
    },
    "c14": {
      "anchor": "10-why",
      "kind": "section",
      "label": "10. Why it's built this way",
      "excerpt": "10. Why it's built this way",
      "text": "show link here as well, links are cheap.\nDon't make me think!  or look!",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:15:14.037Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "the survey, linked above",
      "offset": 20,
      "edited": "2026-11-05T13:15:29.648Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:29:22.852Z",
          "html": "<p>The Why section now links the survey by name, right there. It was the only \"linked above\"; the two \"section 7 / section 8\" pointers in Saying where are links too now.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:47.667Z"
      }
    },
    "c15": {
      "anchor": "10-why#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "10. Why it's built this way",
      "excerpt": "REST Countries is read from where it keeps its data, pinned: its own v3.1 is gon",
      "text": "if some support if-none-match, make language for this?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:16:22.738Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "not with an If-None-Match of ours: swapi refuses that header from a page",
      "offset": 617,
      "edited": "2026-11-05T13:16:30.009Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:32:10.643Z",
          "html": "<p>Every server that sends a version (an ETag) is already asked that way:  the browser sends <code>If-None-Match</code> itself when we read past its cache;  only a header the page writes is refused.  A line anyway, or none: <a href=\"/epics/data/data.plan.html#q39\">Q39</a> (recommended:  none).</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:48.758Z"
      }
    },
    "c16": {
      "anchor": "10-why#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "10. Why it's built this way",
      "excerpt": "REST Countries is read from where it keeps its data, pinned: its own v3.1 is gon",
      "text": "unclear what this means",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:16:54.960Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "A field that's a thing's id is still a field of its own: once it was only the id, a GitHub user's login and a Wikipedia page's title read empty.",
      "offset": 937,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:29:23.979Z",
          "html": "<p>Rewritten: \"A field a server names its things by is still a field you can read.\" Some servers name a thing by one of its fields, not a number; Spell uses that field as the id but keeps it readable too. Once it didn't, so GitHub's <code>its login</code> showed nothing.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:49.826Z"
      }
    },
    "c17": {
      "anchor": "10-why#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "10. Why it's built this way",
      "excerpt": "REST Countries is read from where it keeps its data, pinned: its own v3.1 is gon",
      "text": "unclear",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:17:17.034Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "A list under a thing takes only what it was answered with, never a thing added here: nothing says which thing a new one is under, so a new comment would join every post's comments.",
      "offset": 1082,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:29:25.098Z",
          "html": "<p>Rewritten: \"A list kept under each thing shows only what the server answered.\" A new thing says nothing about which thing it's under, so it would have joined every thing's list; JSONPlaceholder's post comments are the example, and a <code>where</code> over the whole list is the way to see a new one at once.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:50.921Z"
      }
    },
    "c18": {
      "anchor": "10-why#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "10. Why it's built this way",
      "excerpt": "REST Countries is read from where it keeps its data, pinned: its own v3.1 is gon",
      "text": "unclear.  This text assumes that I know the shape of each server, which I don't.  Re-cast text as generic, using site as an example.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:17:41.087Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "The Met's search is its own words: every artwork matching the search.",
      "offset": 1263,
      "edited": "2026-11-05T13:18:03.779Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:29:26.189Z",
          "html": "<p>Rewritten generically: some servers search everything about a thing and answer only ids; <code>matching</code> hands the text to that search, where a <code>where</code> on titles would read every result and drop tag matches. The Met is now just the example. REST Countries' and the search-check bullets got the same treatment; the If-None-Match one is left to C15's thread.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:52.087Z"
      }
    },
    "c19": {
      "anchor": "10-why#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "10. Why it's built this way",
      "excerpt": "REST Countries is read from where it keeps its data, pinned: its own v3.1 is gon",
      "text": "huh?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:18:24.917Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "A property set to one saved thing is read through with",
      "offset": 1527,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:29:27.294Z",
          "html": "<p>Rewritten: in the code Spell writes, a property holding one saved thing is read carefully (<code>this.page?.title</code>), since it may be empty; a check just before drops that, except inside a drawing's function, which runs again later.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:53.167Z"
      }
    },
    "c20": {
      "anchor": "11-limits#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "11. Limits",
      "excerpt": "Not yet: manual tests on real accounts: a Google Sheet, Airtable, an AT Protocol",
      "text": "fix",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-05T13:18:52.455Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "A date shows its day in UTC but its hour in local time.",
      "offset": 592,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:30:23.405Z",
          "html": "<p>Fixed (I73):  a date's day and its hour now come from the same clock, the reader's.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A date on a page shows its day where you are, as <code>the hour of</code> already did.</li>\n  <li>A day alone (<code>\"1977-05-25\"</code>) reads as that day's midnight where you are, not UTC's.</li>\n  <li>Both cores, the JavaScript one and the Python one.</li>\n  <li>Open-Meteo's tab in Los Angeles:  <code>November 6, 2026 · 16:00</code> is now <code>November 5, 2026 · 16:00</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The limit is gone from the guide.</p>",
          "commit": "b1c764a9f"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-05T13:33:13.106Z",
          "html": "<p>Fixed (I73):  a date's day and its hour now come from the same clock, the reader's.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A date on a page shows its day where you are, as <code>the hour of</code> already did.</li>\n  <li>A day alone (<code>\"1977-05-25\"</code>) reads as that day's midnight where you are, not UTC's.</li>\n  <li>Both cores, the JavaScript one and the Python one.</li>\n  <li>Open-Meteo's tab in Los Angeles:  <code>November 6, 2026 · 16:00</code> is now <code>November 5, 2026 · 16:00</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The limit is gone from the guide.</p>",
          "commit": "69053b394"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-05T13:21:54.231Z"
      }
    },
